<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miriam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miriam is described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> as the daughter of Amram and Jochebed and the older sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> and Aaron. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> calls her a prophetess, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> names her among the seven major female prophets of Israel. Tradition identifies her with the unnamed sister who watched the infant Moses on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. In Exodus she leads the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-sea">Song of the Sea</a>. In Numbers she and Aaron speak against Moses over his &quot;Cushite&quot; wife, and she is afflicted with a skin disease and shut out of the camp for seven days. The Torah records that she died and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> also has her follow the infant Moses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miriam.jpg" alt="Miriam" width="330" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Window: Edward Burne-Jones 1886; Photo: Wojciech Dittwald · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E.Burne-Jones_Miriam_St.Giles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
